  "id": "materialism-and-empirio-criticism",
  "title": "Materialism and Empirio-criticism",
  "author": "V.I. Lenin",
  "year": "1908",
  "difficulty": "Hard",
  "readingTime": "14 hours",
  "coverGradient": "linear-gradient(135deg, #8c1c1c 0%, #3d0c0c 100%)",
  "marxistsOrgUrl": "https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1908/mec/index.htm",
  "context": "Written by Lenin between February and October 1908 during his Geneva exile following the defeat of the 1905 Russian Revolution, this major philosophical work combats subjective idealist and neo-Kantian tendencies (Machism, Empirio-criticism, and Bogadanov's Empirio-monism) that infected the Russian Social-Democratic party under the guise of 'modernizing' Marxism with contemporary physics.",
  "relatesToToday": "Essential for refuting subjective idealism, postmodern agnosticism, and philosophical skepticism, establishing the objective reality of the material universe and the criterion of practice in human knowledge.",
  "keyTakeaways": [
    "Philosophy is divided into two irreconcilable fundamental camps: materialism (proceeding from things to sensation) and idealism (proceeding from sensation to things).",
    "Matter is a philosophical category designating the objective reality existing independently of human consciousness and sensation.",
    "The criterion of practice is first and fundamental in epistemology: human social practice proves the objective truth of scientific knowledge.",
    "Scientific crises in physics (such as the discovery of radioactivity and the electron) do not prove that matter has disappeared, but only that previous mechanical limits of our physical knowledge have dissolved.",
    "Philosophy is thoroughly partisan: philosophical debates in epistemology ultimately reflect the class struggle between contending social classes."
  ],
